# UI/UX principles

Goal: Leetify-like clarity for a scouting dossier — verdict first, calm and readable.
Full palette, tokens, and type scale: `docs/DESIGN_SYSTEM.md`.

## Clarity & ease of use
- Verdict first, evidence second; progressive disclosure, max 2 levels (NN/g).
- Level 1 must complete the core task without opening anything; never hide decision-critical info (grade, recommendation, confidence).
- Aesthetic & minimalist: only task-essential content on the first screen.
- Consistency: one shared percentile bar, one callout pattern, one chip style everywhere.
- Always show system status: loading skeletons, empty, and error states are designed, not afterthoughts.

## Defined style
- Use semantic design tokens, never raw hex in components (Material 3 / role-based token model).
- Tiers: `surface`, `content`, `border`, `accent`, `fit` (semantic status). Nothing outside these.
- Exactly one accent color. Fit colors (green/amber/red) only for fit signals, never decoration.
- Never rely on color alone — pair fit signals with text/icon (WCAG 1.4.1).

## Accessibility (WCAG 2.2 AA)
- Body text contrast ≥ 4.5:1; large text (≥24px, or 19px bold) and UI/graphics/focus indicators ≥ 3:1.
- Every `on-*` token must pass contrast against its paired surface.
- Visible focus states; full keyboard operability; honor `prefers-reduced-motion`.

## Responsive (mobile-first, MDN)
- Include `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">`.
- Base styles target mobile; add complexity with `min-width` media queries.
- Prefer fluid layout (Grid/Flexbox, `minmax`, `clamp()`) and relative units (rem/em/%/vw); breakpoints in `em`/`rem`, not device pixels.
- Use container queries for component-level adaptation where it beats viewport queries.
